so for this week's classic interview I  want to go back to an interview that's  now almost 5 years old it's from 2014  with Bill Blunden who's a cyber security  and surveillance expert because of the  recent change to Julian Assange is  status not having Asylum anymore from  the Ecuadorian Embassy in London with  discussions of WikiLeaks cyber hacking  Russia the Trump campaign 2016  bill Blunden when I interviewed him back  in 2014 had extraordinary insights into  a lot of these issues and sort of more  general or overarching ways that I think  are very very interesting to go back to  you so let's do that now  my interview with Bill Blunden from May  of 2014  take a look bill Blunden joins me today  he's an independent computer security  researcher also author of the book  behold a pale farce cyber war threat  inflation and the malware industrial  complex bill it's great to talk to you  and thanks for being here this is just  generally an area where legitimate fears  and hyperbole seem to mix and sometimes  it's hard to tell what's what so let's  start with maybe the broadest question  when it comes to cyber warfare and  malware what really are the most  pressing risks that the average everyday  American should be aware of well I  definitely think that there is an awful  lot of cyber crime I mean if you look at  the target breach recently I believe it  cost targets something like 200 million  dollars to recover from that and there's  also espionage obviously some of it  we're involved in and there's sabotage  too if you look at Stuxnet you know we  sabotage their uh their Iranian  enrichment facility so there's I would  say there's lots of cyber crime there's  espionage there's sabotage and while  they they aren't existential problems I  think there's serious problems and they  would they need to be addressed and I  think on a day-to-day basis those are  the real threats the average person is  going to face so let's talk about some  of the doomsday scenarios that maybe  people have heard about in fact some  that some guests on our program have  talked about in more detail so these  would be a total takedown of the elect  the electrical grid for the country or  parts of the country attacking the  banking system in a way  that would make everyday transactions  impossible financial markets and  significantly affecting the everyday of  financial transactions that were  involved in how real are those threats  in your opinion I think from the  standpoint of statistical analysis there  is no data on any of this with you know  there are plenty of metaphors they use  they use terms like cyber 9-1-1 cyber  katrina cyber war they use terms like a  cyber armageddon or cyber getting  shortened and like I said there's no  data on this so it's hard to really make  a solid prediction at least from a  quantitative standpoint but the people  who are promoting these ideas they sent  up they tend to focus on how bad it'll  be when it happens and I think this is  their tell that this is not intended  this message isn't intended to appeal to  reason it's there to appeal to emotion  it's there to incite an emotional  response it's there to elicit anxiety  and to kind of foment a crisis mentality  this is code was called threat inflation  this is the it's a it's a propaganda  technique that's being used and we saw  it before in the Iraq war when President  Bush and other White House officials  talked about The Smoking Gun that came  in the form of mushroom cloud and when  you get someone who's in an anxious  position who's you know kind of in a  crisis mentality they'll pay any amount  of money to be safe but if you think  about a rational person even though  there are several types of insurance you  could buy there's a whole there's  countless types of insurance but you  don't go out and buy every type of  insurance you carefully compute what  your risk is to each threat and then you  make a cost-benefit analysis and you  decide carefully how you're gonna  protect yourself and when you're in this  crisis mentality when you're anxious and  you you've been kind of subjected to  threat inflation you're in a position  where you can waste a lot of resources  and yield up your civil liberties and  try to protect yourself at a very high  cost when and in doing so neglect these  genuine threats that we face on a  day-to-day basis I think that the actual  threat of cyber Armageddon is  pretty low and a lot of officials agree  with this the people who run the grid  organizations the North American  Electric Reliability corporation the the  the chief executive that organization  says we're not worried about the cyber  threat we're far more worried about the  physical threats and and that would make  sense because they're actually al are a  lot of instances where they're in  physical instances that brought down  what brought down power and the same  thing with the banking industry I think  that people don't give the banking  industry enough credit large financial  institutions like the Federal Reserve  they have all sorts of elaborate  disaster recovery measures in place and  they go to lengths to great lengths they  have professionals who are dedicated to  keeping these networks safe and to think  that you know that you're gonna you know  disable our financial institutions they  keep backups you know and they keep them  in vaults under ground I think to to  stop an institution like the Federal  Reserve to really grind it to a halt  over the over a long term you'd probably  need a nuclear for a strike  so you mentioned something interesting  there which was the amount that you're  willing to give up in terms of privacy  or civil liberties seems to be directly  correlated to how afraid or anxious you  are about a particular scenario and  we've talked about this quite a bit  recently in light of the revelations  from Edward Snowden which if you had  paid attention to the passing of the  Patriot Act and if you saw the level of  fear and thus what Americans were  willing to accept in the immediate  aftermath of 9/11 you wouldn't really  have been that surprised by the  revelations of Edward Snowden which  kinds of kind of gets us to another area  I wanted to ask you about we often hear  the response to news that there is  surveillance being done of Americans of  listen I'm confident I'm not doing  anything wrong and therefore I just  don't really care go ahead and spy on me  I don't really care it makes no  difference to me in general my responses  to that is we'll listen  we have a certain level of over  criminalization in this country and even  if you truly believe you're not really  doing anything wrong having this  information in the hands of different  government agencies may at some point in  the future who even knows what laws you  may be breaking or may  have retro actively broken at some point  and that makes me very cautious but I'm  wondering are there better arguments  against those who say hey I'm doing  nothing wrong I don't care I think that  we need to understand very clearly that  these civil liberties are safeguards  against oppression against despots and  they the people who created who founded  them believed enough to die for them and  anyone who's who's grown up my wife grew  up in Beijing during the Cultural  Revolution she knows what it's like to  live in a police state anyone who lived  in Russia during the reign of Stalin  understands why you need these why you  need these liberties why they're  important because you don't know how  much you miss them until they're taken  away and I think to an extent we're  starting to see signs of the same  illness here in United States for  example the 2012 National Defense  Authorization Act has detainment  provisions which are very unsettling for  journalists and activists who could be  deemed cooperating with terrorists  somehow and be detained without any sort  of due process I think that you know  this hole right now we we exist in a  surveillance state it's I have no doubt  in my own mind that we have you know the  corporate players and the government's  the government spies had put in place a  mechanism where they can monitor almost  everything at real-time and and to to  assume that our secret policies you know  which are judged by by courts that are  secret and and issue mandates their  secret to believe that this secret  mechanism will safeguard us from devolve  into a police state I think is really  the height of recklessness I think it's  really it's a huge threat to our to our  democratic republic see it's interesting  because I agree with a lot of what  you're saying bill and the issue I have  and I asked this not in a rhetorical  sense but this is really an internal  discussion that we have all the time on  the program which is on the one hand I  generally tend to agree with the ideas  yours  pausing when it comes to surveillance  although maybe in a little bit less of a  I don't know apocalyptic sense but at  the same time there's a crossover  between the beliefs you've just espoused  with regard to surveillance and the  extreme pro-gun Second Amendment  gun-toting enthusiasts that are  supporting all sorts of laws that I  think are quite destructive in different  states around the country do you see the  two sides as one battle so to speak or  do you see how the two may be different  I think that if you wanted to really  draw a distinction between the civil  libertarians and the libertarians it has  to do with the ultimately the role of  government obviously libertarians they  believe they can exist without  government and my response to that is if  you want to see what it's like to live  in a society without any government go  hang out in the tribal regions of  Pakistan for a little while the civil  libertarians they believe that  governments actually it should be there  as a safeguard against people things  like people dumping toxic waste for  example or the banks who are running  rampant right now ostensibly the  government center to safeguard us  obviously it's not doing a very good job  with that  mm-hmm but I think in terms of civil  libertarians and libertarians you have a  basically right left kind of it has to  do the difference between the two has to  do with role of government but I think  there is some common ground in terms of  the idea of are we going to trade  Liberty for security and there was an  op-ed piece that was written by Tom  Friedman in the New York Times several  months back it was called blowing the  whistle and he basically said his  argument was that we should give up a  little bit of our Liberty our Liberty  just give up a little dissolute we can  prevent an attack which will end up  taking away all of our freedoms so this  attack will be so great this terrorist  attack would be so apocalyptic that it  will it will end all of our other  freedoms and we'll end up you know in a  really bad situation so he said I'd be  willing to give up a little  a little liberty out front to to  safeguard ourselves against such an  attack and I think there was enough  negative response that Bill Keller  actually wrote a second op-ed shortly  afterwards reinforcing and trying to  bolster Tom Friedman's position but  ultimately they're both saying that we  should undermine the Constitution to  save it and it reminds me of Iraq where  we said we have to destroy Iraq in order  to save it and I think that even our  founding fathers disagreed with this  Benjamin Franklin for example said those  who would yield essential Liberty on  behalf of a little temporary security  deserve neither liberty or security hmm  and I I agree I think in this case you  know it's not terrorism that destroys  freedom it's our response to terrorism  that destroys freedom the British  government back during World War two  when they were being bombed by the  Germans they would hang these signs out  that said remain calm carry on right and  I think that's what we need to do I  think if you look at how Japan handled  the nerve gas attacks by the Amish and  Riccio did they rely on indefinite  detention did they torture people did  they use mass interception did they  suspend civil liberties they didn't do  any of that no they had their police  conduct investigations they indicted  people and they tried them in a civilian  court very simple very straightforward  they remain calm and they carried on and  I think that's what we need to do as a  country very interesting the distinction  between civil libertarians and  libertarians definitely something we're  going to explore further the book is  behold a pale farce bill blundin thanks  so much for joining us today thank you  David

